## 2. FOUR VOICE MODES

Implement the following modes through the existing interface without creating a new dashboard.

### Mode A: Push to Talk

Let me click the microphone button, speak, and submit my command.

Stop recognition when the interaction finishes or I explicitly stop the session.

Display the finalized transcript so I can review or edit it before submission when appropriate.

### Mode B: Hands-Free Conversation — PRIMARY MODE

This is the main experience I want.

After I explicitly start a session, ProofLoop should listen, understand, execute, respond aloud, and continue listening without requiring another microphone click after every response.

Example:

Me: "Prepare my internship application."

ProofLoop: "Sure. Which company are you applying to?"

Me: "Microsoft."

ProofLoop: "Which role are you applying for?"

Me: "Software development intern."

ProofLoop: "I'll prepare the application with those details."

ProofLoop should retain the conversation context, understand follow-up answers, and ask only necessary clarification questions.

Use the following interaction cycle:

LISTEN → FINALIZE TRANSCRIPT → UNDERSTAND → EXECUTE → SPEAK RESPONSE → RESUME LISTENING.

Pause or suppress recognition while the assistant is speaking unless the selected engine provides reliable simultaneous listening and echo control.

Provide an explicit Stop Conversation control. Stopping the session must immediately stop recognition and must not automatically restart it.

### Mode C: Optional Wake Phrase

Where genuinely supported, provide an optional configurable wake phrase such as "Hey ProofLoop."

After detecting the wake phrase, recognize the user's command and send it through the existing agent.

First determine whether the selected browser and speech engine can reliably support wake-phrase detection.

Do not pretend that a continuously running wake-word engine exists if it has not been implemented.

If continuous wake-word detection is not supported, clearly indicate that limitation and provide Hands-Free Conversation and Push to Talk as alternatives.

Do not attempt to bypass browser permissions, operating-system restrictions, or microphone security controls.

### Mode D: Background Workflow Continuation

When I start a supported workflow, its backend operations should continue independently of the microphone session whenever the existing backend architecture supports this.

I should be able to stop voice listening or navigate to another ProofLoop route without accidentally canceling a running backend workflow.

Preserve the workflow identifier, conversation context, and actual task status.

When voice is active again, the assistant should be able to explain the workflow's real progress and result.

Do not claim continuous microphone access or spoken notifications when the browser has suspended the tab, the browser has closed, or audio delivery is unavailable.

